<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959)</h1><p class="st-meta">1902 CE – 1959 CE · capital Havana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Independent Cuba under US tutelage, a sugar republic of dictators and reformers that ended when Fidel Castro's revolution drove out Batista.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-states-military-government-in-cuba">United States Military Government in Cuba</a> ended, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-cuba">Republic of Cuba</a> was inaugurated on 20 May 1902 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-estrada-palma">Tomás Estrada Palma</a> as president. Its constitution included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platt-amendment">Platt Amendment</a>, which allowed US intervention, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington</a> soon used it, occupying the island again from 1906 to 1909.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a>, largely owned by American firms, dominated the economy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> became a magnet for tourists and gamblers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerardo-machado">Gerardo Machado's</a> dictatorship from 1925 ended in revolution in 1933, after which the army sergeant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulgencio-batista">Fulgencio Batista</a> became the power behind governments. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platt-amendment">Platt Amendment</a> was repealed in 1934, and a progressive constitution was adopted in 1940. Batista served as elected president from 1940 to 1944, and corruption marked the governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-grau-san-martin">Grau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-prio-socarras">Prío</a> that followed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulgencio-batista">Batista</a> seized power in a coup in 1952. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a> attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-the-moncada-barracks">Moncada barracks</a> in 1953 and returned on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granma-yacht">Granma</a> in 1956 to wage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-maestra">Sierra Maestra</a>. Batista fled on 1 January 1959, and the revolutionary <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-cuba">Republic of Cuba</a> began.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 1902 CE – 1959 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:97.55%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1902 CE – 1959 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Havana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tomás Estrada Palma, Gerardo Machado, Fulgencio Batista</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cuba">Cuba</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Cuba, First Cuban Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=republic-of-cuba-1902-1959" aria-label="Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2481 1324 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2801 1580L2801 1580M2801 1580L2570 1008M2801 1580L2801 1580M2801 1580L2801 1580"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2801 1580h0M2570 1008h0M2801 1580h0M280